WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.910
Build a cost estimate from the resource lifecycle, not the advertised GPU rate.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.869
Compute charges accumulate while an instance is running,

00:00:07.869 --> 00:00:11.939
including time spent installing software, downloading models, waiting for a user,

00:00:11.939 --> 00:00:14.529
or sitting idle after a job completes.

00:00:14.529 --> 00:00:17.118
Persistent disks, snapshots, object storage, reserved addresses,

00:00:17.118 --> 00:00:20.818
and logs may continue to cost money after compute stops.

00:00:20.818 --> 00:00:23.778
Data transfer charges can appear when models, datasets,

00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:26.737
or results cross regions or leave the provider.

00:00:26.737 --> 00:00:29.697
On-demand capacity offers flexibility with no long commitment.

00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:34.137
Reserved or committed capacity may reduce unit price for predictable use but

00:00:34.137 --> 00:00:36.356
transfers utilization risk to the customer.

00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:40.796
Spot or interruptible capacity can be economical for restartable batch jobs, experiments,

00:00:40.796 --> 00:00:41.906
and checkpointed training.

00:00:41.906 --> 00:00:46.345
It is a poor fit for work that cannot tolerate termination or

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:48.565
for an interactive service without failover.

00:00:48.565 --> 00:00:51.895
Compare effective completed-work cost, not only the hourly discount.

00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:54.854
Shutdown discipline is the most direct cost control.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:57.814
Tag resources with owner, project, purpose, and expiration.

00:00:57.814 --> 00:01:01.514
Use automatic idle detection carefully, scheduled shutdowns for development environments,

00:01:01.514 --> 00:01:05.213
and job automation that releases compute after success or failure.

00:01:05.213 --> 00:01:10.023
Distinguish stopping from deleting: a stopped instance may retain billable disks and addresses.

00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:14.832
Define retention needs before cleanup so cost control does not destroy required data.

00:01:14.832 --> 00:01:18.162
Set budgets and alerts at account and project levels,

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:23.342
but do not treat alerts as hard limits unless the platform explicitly enforces them.

00:01:23.342 --> 00:01:27.041
Review actual charges against estimates and investigate unusual runtime, transfer,

00:01:27.041 --> 00:01:28.151
or storage growth.

00:01:28.151 --> 00:01:32.591
Record cost per completed job or per useful request alongside technical performance.

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:36.660
The cheapest GPU hour is not economical if setup overhead, interruptions,

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.620
or poor model fit require many more hours.
